.TH XFILTER 1 "September 2026" "xfilter 0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xfilter \- policy-enforcing X11 proxy for untrusted clients
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xfilter
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ command " ...]"
.br
.B xfilter
.B \-\-ssh
.I host
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ "remote command" " ...]"
.br
.B xfilter
.BR \-\-domain " | " \-\-use " | " \-\-env " | " \-\-stop
.I name
.br
.B xfilter \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xfilter
sits between an X client and the real X server and puts every request to a
policy before forwarding it.  It runs on the machine with the real X server
\(em the one you sit at.  Nothing is installed on the remote host, and the
remote side gets no say in what is filtered.
.PP
The policy is default\-deny: a request is passed only if it is on the safe
allowlist or a rule decides on it.  Reads of other clients' data are refused,
the handful of foreign reads that theming and window management need are kept,
CLIPBOARD and PRIMARY are gated, and anything nobody thought of is blocked and
logged rather than passed on trust.  A refused request is never simply dropped
\(em the server counts requests to generate sequence numbers \(em so denials
are substituted with something harmless and the client sees a plausible
answer rather than a broken stream.
.PP
Clients reach the proxy over a display of its own, with a cookie of its own:
they never hold the cookie for the real server.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-display " DISPLAY"
Display to offer clients.  Default: the first free one from
.BR :20 .
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " HOST"
Run
.B ssh \-X
to
.I HOST
through the proxy; anything after
.B \-\-
is the remote command.
.TP
.BI \-\-upstream " UPSTREAM"
The real display to forward to.  Default:
.BR $DISPLAY .
The
.B \-\-gate ask
prompt opens here too, deliberately not on the filtered display.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " FILE"
Authority file (xauth format) holding the cookie clients must present to
.BR \-\-display ;
created if missing.  Default: a private temporary file, removed on exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-upstream\-auth " FILE"
Authority file holding the cookie for
.BR \-\-upstream .
Default:
.BR $XAUTHORITY ,
then
.BR ~/.Xauthority .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report every connection and its outcome.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what the policy would refuse, refusing nothing.  Without it the policy
is enforced.
.TP
.BI \-\-gate " {deny,allow,ask}"
What a filtered application may do with CLIPBOARD or PRIMARY \(em both reading
one and taking ownership of one, which is how it offers a copy out.
.B deny
(the default) means copying out of the forwarded application does not work
either;
.B allow
passes both;
.B ask
prompts you, showing the value and which application wants it, and is the
setting that gives you a clipboard in both directions.
.TP
.BI \-\-gate\-timeout " S"
Deny a pending prompt after this long.  Default: 20 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-gate\-remember " S"
How long \(lqAllow for a while\(rq lasts.  Default: 300 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " NAME"
Run as the filter for this trust domain: the display and the cookie are
derived from the name, so nothing has to be remembered or passed around.
Runs in the foreground and lives until it is stopped; if one is already
running for this name it says so and exits.  Everything behind one filter can
read everything else behind it, so the name is the security boundary: one per
account, or per application you do not trust.
.TP
.BI \-\-use " NAME"
Run the command after
.B \-\-
against this domain's filtered display; the environment is scoped to that
command and nothing else.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " NAME"
Print this domain's two export lines on stdout, for
.B eval
in a shell script.  Everything else goes to stderr, so the output is safe to
eval.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the filtered domains running for you.
.TP
.BI \-\-stop " NAME"
Stop the filter for a domain.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Also append the operation log \(em each new operation, and the exit report \(em
to
.IR FILE .
It always goes to stdout as well.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the option summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
An interactive remote shell, where everything launched from it is filtered:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xfilter \-\-gate ask \-\-ssh remotehost
.fi
.RE
.PP
One remote application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xfilter \-\-gate ask \-\-ssh remotehost \-\- firefox
.fi
.RE
.PP
A trust domain kept up on its own, then used:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xfilter \-\-domain work@buildbox \-\-gate ask &
xfilter \-\-use work@buildbox \-\- ./deploy.sh
xfilter \-\-list
xfilter \-\-stop work@buildbox
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look before you leap: report what the policy would refuse, refusing nothing.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xfilter \-\-dry\-run \-\-ssh remotehost \-\- some\-application
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The real display forwarded to, unless
.B \-\-upstream
says otherwise.
.TP
.B XAUTHORITY
Where the cookie for that display is read from, unless
.B \-\-upstream\-auth
says otherwise.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/xfilter/xfilter.py
The command.
.B /usr/bin/xfilter
and
.B /usr/bin/xfilter.py
are links to it.
.TP
.I /usr/share/xfilter/xfilter_core.py
The relay it is built on: connection setup and cookie swap, request and reply
parsing, atom and extension bookkeeping.  It has to sit beside
.IR xfilter.py .
It also runs on its own as a pure profiler, which denies nothing:
.B python3 /usr/share/xfilter/xfilter_core.py \-\-display :20 ...
.TP
.I /usr/share/xfilter/xfilter.bash
Shell helpers defining
.BR xssh ,
which starts a domain's filter if needed and then sshs through it.  Source it
from
.I ~/.bashrc
to use it.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/xfilter/examples/xfilter@.service
A systemd user template unit, one instance per trust domain.
.SH NOTES
.B \-\-gate ask
needs
.B python3-gi
and
.B gir1.2\-gtk\-3.0
for the prompt and its preview, and a display to draw them on: the dialog
opens on
.BR \-\-upstream ,
never on the filtered display, since routing it through the proxy would put
the dialog's own clipboard reads in front of the gate they exist to serve.
The bindings are checked as the arguments are parsed, so a missing package is
an immediate error rather than a failure once the proxy is running.
.PP
Everything behind one filter can read everything else behind it.  The trust
boundary is the filter, not the application: run a separate one per account,
or per application you do not trust.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR Xsecurity (7),
.BR xauth (1).
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/xfilter/README.md.gz
is the full documentation, and
.I /usr/share/doc/xfilter/AUDIT.md.gz
records what the policy has been attacked with and what it stops.
